---
import LabLayout from '../../layouts/LabLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../consts';

const sections = [
  { id: 'objective', label: 'Objective' },
  { id: 'method', label: 'Method' },
  { id: 'findings', label: 'Findings' },
  { id: 'triage', label: 'Triage' },
  { id: 'remediation', label: 'Remediation' },
  { id: 'lesson', label: 'Lesson' },
];
---

<LabLayout
  title="Metasploitable 2: assessment, not a score"
  subtitle="OpenVAS, Nikto, and Netcat produce a lot of noise. The skill is triage."
  kicker="Assessment / Metasploitable"
  description="A vulnerability assessment and service enumeration exercise against an isolated Metasploitable 2 virtual machine using OpenVAS, Nikto, Nmap, and Netcat."
  meta={[
    { label: 'Environment', value: 'Kali Linux · VirtualBox' },
    { label: 'Target', value: 'Metasploitable 2 (isolated)' },
    { label: 'Tools', value: 'OpenVAS · Nikto · Nmap · Netcat' },
    { label: 'Date', value: '2026-07-16' },
  ]}
  sections={sections}
  repoUrl={`${SITE.githubUrl}/6-metasploitable-probe`}
  outcomes={[
    { value: '1', label: 'OpenVAS report' },
    { value: '1', label: 'Nikto report' },
    { value: '4', label: 'tools used' },
    { value: '0', label: 'production systems' },
  ]}
>
  <section id="objective" class="article-section">
    <h2>Objective</h2>
    <p>
      Practice a full assessment workflow against a deliberately vulnerable VM: enumerate
      services, scan for known weaknesses, inspect the web tier, grab banners, and turn the
      output into a prioritized remediation list.
    </p>
    <p>
      The target was Metasploitable 2 on an isolated host-only network. The output is
      intentionally not a list of exploits. The goal was assessment and triage.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="method" class="article-section">
    <h2>Method</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>OpenVAS / Greenbone.</strong> Ran a vulnerability scan and exported the
        report with severity, CVSS scores, affected services, and recommended mitigations.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Nmap.</strong> Enumerated services and versions to build the target map.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Nikto.</strong> Assessed the Apache Tomcat service on port 8180 for
        misconfigurations and exposed paths.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Netcat.</strong> Grabbed service banners to confirm what was listening and
        what it claimed to be.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="article-code">
      nikto -h http://192.168.55.198:8180 -o nikto-report.html -Format html
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="findings" class="article-section">
    <h2>Findings</h2>
    <p>
      OpenVAS produced the broadest view: a set of findings with severity ratings, CVSS
      scores, affected services, and remediation guidance. Nikto flagged web server
      misconfigurations and exposed paths on the Tomcat instance. Banner grabbing confirmed
      the service versions behind the ports.
    </p>
    <p>
      The raw reports are the evidence. This note focuses on how I read them, because a
      scanner report without triage is just a longer list of open ports.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="triage" class="article-section">
    <h2>Triage</h2>
    <p>
      I sorted findings by three questions: Is the service reachable from a network that
      matters? Is the weakness exploitable without unusual preconditions? What is the impact
      if it is? A high CVSS score on a service nobody can reach is less urgent than a medium
      finding on an exposed management interface.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Exposure:</strong> which network can reach the service?</li>
      <li><strong>Exploitability:</strong> does it require credentials, user interaction, or a rare configuration?</li>
      <li><strong>Impact:</strong> confidentiality, integrity, availability — and whose data?</li>
      <li><strong>Confidence:</strong> did I validate the scanner’s claim, or just repeat it?</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="remediation" class="article-section">
    <h2>Remediation</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Patch or upgrade the affected services; remove default applications.</li>
      <li>Restrict Tomcat and management interfaces to the lab network only.</li>
      <li>Disable unused services and close unnecessary ports.</li>
      <li>Harden the web tier: remove sample apps, restrict verbs, review error pages.</li>
      <li>Run services with least privilege and monitor for unexpected changes.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="lesson" class="article-section">
    <h2>Lesson</h2>
    <p>
      Scanners are inputs, not conclusions. The valuable work is validating a finding,
      understanding exposure, and writing a fix that someone can actually apply. A report
      that says “critical” without context is not an assessment.
    </p>
  </section>
</LabLayout>
